A hard line break is two or more spaces at the end of a line. A reader
SHOWS that break, thus it is not the same as a soft break, which a reader
turns into a space.

The input holds the two spaces, and they are invisible. `wc -c` says 20
bytes. Do not retype that line.

`md format` writes the break back with a backslash. Both spellings mean
the same break, and the backslash is the one that lives through a tool
that takes off trailing space.
